{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-34313",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis. Let us call ${\\theta }^{′}$ the acute angle that is formed with the $x$-axis such that ${\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘}=\\theta $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196632/assets/d14f62179f831ebdc177eac7bd2899679864305d4104d4b61f304eb4fa9e133d.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n{\\theta }^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using $\\theta ={\\theta }^{′}+{180}^{∘},$ we get $\\theta ={30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={210}^{∘}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
